# Trial-division primality test using NEGATION-AS-FAILURE -- the textbook
# formulation: N is prime iff no candidate divides it.
#
# Requires stratified rule evaluation (zelph >= 0.9.8): the ¬-rule below is
# deferred until the positive rules -- candidate enumeration and all mod
# computations -- have reached quiescence, so the negation tests absence
# against the complete divisor scan.
#
# .strata lists that rule as not stratifiable, together with the whole
# arithmetic. The analysis compares predicates, and the verdict rule (N
# testprime N, N isprime N) => ((N testprime N) = prime) creates an = fact,
# the predicate for each arithmetic result the scan reads, and contains (N
# testprime N), which starts the scan. Neither can produce a fact upon
# which the negation relies: the = fact has (N testprime N) as its subject,
# never one of the arithmetic terms the scan reads, and (N testprime N) is
# already present, as the rule's own premise. Thus, every hasdivisor fact
# for N is derived before the negation is tested, and .semi-naive check
# verifies it at every execution.
# 
# Companion script: primes.zph solves
# the same problem WITHOUT negation via a positive fold, which doubles as
# a lazy scheduler (early exit at the smallest divisor). This script scans
# eagerly up to sqrt(N) -- the negation needs the full scan -- and in
# return finds ALL divisors <= sqrt(N), not just the smallest.

# Default arithmetic substrate; import a different provider of
# "arithmetic" (or claim the ID via .provides) BEFORE this module to override:
.import binary-arithmetic

(N testprime N) => (&2 cmp N)
(N testprime N, &2 == N) => (N isprime N)
(N testprime N, &2 < N) => (&2 divisorcand N)

(D divisorcand N) => (D + &1)
(D divisorcand N, (D + &1) = E) => (E * E)
(D divisorcand N, (D + &1) = E, (E * E) = P) => (P cmp N)
(D divisorcand N, (D + &1) = E, (E * E) = P, P < N) => (E divisorcand N)
(D divisorcand N, (D + &1) = E, (E * E) = P, P == N) => (E divisorcand N)

(D divisorcand N) => (N mod D)
(D divisorcand N, (N mod D) = &0) => (N hasdivisor D)

# The textbook rule -- sound as written (the header explains the reason for
# .strata's disagreement):
(N testprime N, &2 < N, ¬(N hasdivisor D)) => (N isprime N)

(N testprime N, N isprime N) => ((N testprime N) = prime)
(N testprime N, N hasdivisor D) => ((N testprime N) = composite)
